703,"knowledgeEvents":[],"knowledgeEstimateBasis":"Potential one-read saving only: one read per referenced file, capped at 6,000 tokens each, minus the delivered note block. Sizes use 3.6 characters per token (source bytes). No repeat-read multiplier or dollar credit. Actual avoided reading and causal savings were not measured.","judgment":{"score":1,"samples":[1],"reasons":["This matches the merged patch functionally. Creating a shared metric now always assigns a fresh uuid unless the metric already exists, the same as upstream. For updates that send only saved_metrics_ids, the agent finds stored inline lists whose uuids clash with the attached shared metrics and regenerates those uuids. Upstream does this by copying the lists into update_data before validation; the agent does it after validation with deterministic uuid5 values. Either way the inline metric gets a new uuid and the shared metric keeps its own. The ordering sync removes shared-metric uuids of the same type from the 'removed' set, as upstream does. The guidance file was rewritten so it no longer recommends promoting a metric and keeping the inline copy. The behavior is equivalent."],"reason":"This matches the merged patch functionally. Creating a shared metric now always assigns a fresh uuid unless the metric already exists, the same as upstream. For updates that send only saved_metrics_ids, the agent finds stored inline lists whose uuids clash with the attached shared metrics and regenerates those uuids. Upstream does this by copying the lists into update_data before validation; the agent does it after validation with deterministic uuid5 values. Either way the inline metric gets a new uuid and the shared metric keeps its own. The ordering sync removes shared-metric uuids of the same type from the 'removed' set, as upstream does. The guidance file was rewritten so it no longer recommends promoting a metric and keeping the inline copy. The behavior is equivalent.","mustHit":3,"mustTotal":3,"touched":["